  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhance HIV prevention services in Tanzania - Tanzania (United Republic) (01-May-2013-30-Apr-2016)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government of Tanzania&#x2019;s commitment to equitable delivery of quality health services faces major challenges relating to operating without the necessary skilled staffing levels, an inability to recruit and retain as many health workers as are required, low training capacity, poor procurement systems, and a disintegrated health information system.   This Program seeks to address these challenges by increasing health workforce production; improving workforce attraction and retention; strengthening use of quality health information systems for planning and monitoring; strengthening procurement, storage, and quality assurance of medical products; and improving system governance and stewardship. These interventions are based on the country&#x2019;s national strategies to strengthen the health system and the response to HIV, TB and malaria. They address four of the World Health Organization&#x2019;s six key health systems building blocks, namely, the health workforce; information systems; medical products and vaccines and technology; and leadership and governance. The Program seeks to build on the work of the Tanzanian Government and development partners and the Program seeks to fill in the gaps in pledged and on-going support.</narrative>
  </description>
